  "full_response": "The journey of cloud computing is a fascinating evolution from monolithic mainframes to today's flexible and efficient serverless architectures. It dates back to the 1960s when mainframe computers like IBM's System/360 became standard in corporations and universities. These centralized machines provided computational services to multiple terminals but were bound by their physical locations and limited scalability.\n\n\nWith advent of the client-server model in the 1970s, computing began to shift away from monolithic mainframes. This decentralized framework allowed different tasks to be handled by separate machines – servers – and presented services to user terminals or thin clients.\n\n\nThe late 1990s marked a significant milestone with the rise of the internet and the dot-com boom. High-speed internet made it possible to transmit data over long distances, which enhanced collaboration and resource sharing. This infrastructure was the perfect breeding ground for the next wave of cloud computing: distributed networks accessed via the web.\n\n\nIn 2006, Amazon Web Services (AWS) launched EC2, making the internet the gateway to virtual private servers for the first time. This open-market model transformed cloud computing into a scalable, on-demand service, encouraging widespread adoption and innovation.\n\n\nDuring the 2010s, cloud computing continued to mature. We saw the rise of Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS), Platform as a Service (PaaS), and Software as a Service (SaaS) models, each offering different levels of abstraction and control. These models improved the fault tolerance, scalability, and cost-efficiency of complex IT infrastructure.\n\n\nServerless computing emerged as a panacea for developers to build and deploy applications without worrying about the underlying server infrastructure. By abstracting the server layer completely, developers can focus on code execution, which the cloud provider manages automatically. Serverless architectures have further transformed the cloud computing landscape, empowering agility and reducing the cost of operations even further. The from mainframes to a world where the backend infrastructure is virtually invisible marks one of the most transformational eras in IT history.",
